INT. MINIATURE GOLF CLUBHOUSE - DAY

A snack-bar-and-trophy-case combo built to look like a pirate ship, with a fibreglass parrot bolted over the register. The carpet is the green of a sick lawn. Rain taps the porthole windows. A hand-lettered banner reads: CAPTAIN HOOK'S INVITATIONAL - FINAL ROUND.

BARNABY PLOTT, sixties, beard dyed an unnatural black, in a captain's hat and a tracksuit, polishes a plastic trophy with a napkin. At the counter, DOLORES "DEE" OKAFOR-LINDQVIST, forties, a brisk accountant in a rain slicker, studies a clipboard. In a booth, young MARCUS VANCE, twenty, wears a bowling shirt and sips a slushie with great solemnity.

DEE
So the prize pool is four hundred dollars.

BARNABY
Four hundred and twelve. Counting the raffle.

DEE
And the entry fees were forty each, eleven entrants.

BARNABY
Correct.

DEE
Then the prize pool is four forty.

BARNABY
Ah. Well. There's the hat.

DEE
Barnaby.

BARNABY
The hat was twenty-eight dollars.

MARCUS
(through straw)
It's a good hat.

DEE
Nobody asked you.

MARCUS
Sorry.

A beat of calm. Barnaby sets the trophy on the shelf. Dee clicks her pen shut. The rain eases. For a moment the whole place feels cosy.

BARNABY
You know, I think this is the best year we've had.

DEE
It might be.

MARCUS
The windmill was running smooth this morning.

BARNABY
That windmill has not run smooth since the Reagan administration.

MARCUS
Well. It did today.

DEE
(still writing)
Who's the other finalist?

BARNABY
Some fellow. Showed up at nine, paid cash. Name on the sheet is... Smith.

DEE
Just Smith.

BARNABY
I assumed he was shy.

The door opens. A TALL STRANGER in a trench coat enters, shaking out an umbrella. Sixty, silver hair, eyes like cold coins. He walks to the counter and sets a putter on it.

STRANGER
I'm here for the final.

BARNABY
Mister Smith! Wonderful. Dee, this is the competition.

STRANGER
It's not Smith.

DEE
Pardon?

STRANGER
It's Vance.

Marcus chokes on his slushie. Barnaby looks from the stranger to Marcus and back. Dee puts down her pen.

MARCUS
Dad?

VANCE
Hello, Marcus.

BARNABY
You two know each other?

MARCUS
He told me he was dead.

VANCE
I told you I was in Arizona.

MARCUS
For nine years.

DEE
Mr. Vance, we don't actually have a rule about estranged fathers, but I think I should say the raffle money is already spent.

BARNABY
Spent on the hat.

DEE
Barnaby.

VANCE
I read about the tournament in the paper. Your name was on the entry list. I thought, he's still playing.

MARCUS
I never stopped.

VANCE
I know. I used to watch you through the fence.

Barnaby takes off the hat and holds it to his chest. Dee's eyes go wide. Nobody speaks. The parrot above the register creaks on its bolt.

MARCUS
(quiet)
Fine. Eighteen holes. Winner buys lunch.

VANCE
And the loser?

MARCUS
Asks the other one a question.

Vance nods once. He picks up his putter. Marcus slides out of the booth with his own, an old one with tape on the grip.

BARNABY
(whispering to Dee)
I think that's the best tournament we've ever had.

DEE
(whispering back)
I'm still short twelve dollars.
